Deliberately not changed: the treatment of a two-symbol alphabet as
binary regardless of the declared sample width (upstream #253 and
PR #256). Until 2026-10-02 this paragraph said "pending NIST" and that NIST reads "binary" as a property of the alphabet; the reading is @joshuaehill's, and NIST has not spoken.
His position (PR #256, 2026-09-30): {0,1} and {33,211} are both
two-symbol alphabets, so the "if the sequential dataset is not binary"
condition of §3.1.3 is not met and no n x H_bitstring term is due. The
fork owner asked the same day whether an explicitly declared
bits_per_symbol of 8 changes that. He answered on 2026-10-01 at 21:38
UTC, after walking through read_file_subset(): the alph_size > 2 gate
tests the translated alphabet, which he holds correct; a declared width
only fixes the encoding of data.bsymbols, which the bitstring
assessment uses when -c forces it; without -c a two-symbol dataset is
already binary and no separate H_bitstring is produced or, under
§3.1.3, wanted. "In summary, I think that this aspect of the current
behavior is correct."

So the question is answered, not open. master follows upstream here:
the gate is unchanged, the patch exists only on branch
fix/bitstring-gate-word-size and in PR #256, and the shipped build
therefore agrees with his reading and reports 0.9216 rather than 0.1796
on the audit's two-valued 2-bit input. What remains is a DECISION for
the owner and the independent review, recorded 2026-10-02 and
deliberately not taken here: either (a) withdraw the branch patch and
PR #256 and record his reading as the fork's, or (b) adopt the patch on
master as a deliberate, documented divergence from upstream, with the
counter-argument stated. The counter-argument, as filed in #253: §3.1.3
makes the bitstring assessment depend on the sample width n, §3.2.2
requires the submitter to document that width, and a comparator or
threshold source captured into bytes (0x00/0xFF) is an n-bit source
that happens to emit two values, so relabelling it to {0,1} drops the
n x H_bitstring term and over-credits it by up to 5.1x on the
reproduction, unbounded in principle. Until the decision is taken the
fork follows upstream's behaviour unchanged.

