Known upstream defects carried into the shipped build, and the exclusion
rule that stopped applying (recorded 2026-10-02)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

The 2026-09-30 audits deliberately did not queue defects that were already
known upstream: they are listed as rediscoveries, "not counted", in
audits/2026-09-30/novel-findings/REPORT.md section 3 and in that directory's
agent-reports/restart.md. That was sound while the rule above held, because
master then carried upstream's behaviour on purpose and an upstream defect was
not this fork's to answer. THAT RATIONALE LAPSED on 2026-09-30, when master
became the patched build and this build became a product dependency. Nothing
recorded the lapse until now, so defects excluded under the old rule were left
out of the queue under the new one.

At least one of them is a memory-safety defect in a program this fork builds
and ships:

  * cpp/restart_main.cpp, simulateBound(): line 124 allocates with
    `new uint16_t[simulation_rounds]` and line 161 frees it with a scalar
    `delete results`. Freeing an array with scalar delete is undefined
    behaviour. The code is upstream's, unchanged here and unchanged since
    2023, and ordinary valid input reaches it. A default sanitizer build does
    not diagnose it; ASAN_OPTIONS=alloc_dealloc_mismatch=1 aborts at line 161
    on the full command line (audits/2026-10-01-re-audit/KNOWN-242.md records
    those runs). Upstream PR #242 by garlic-hub, "Fix warnings and set them to
    be errors", carries `delete[]` in its diff and is open; it is not an issue
    describing this defect, and the re-audit's file name calls it one. Nothing
    in this fork repairs it, and no regression covers it.

It is recorded here rather than silently repaired because changing it is a
decision about diverging further from upstream on a program whose figures no
product prices anything against, and because the honest statement of what
ships must name it. The findings tracker now carries it in the "Other upstream
items" table with the same note.

No continuous integration (recorded 2026-10-02)
-----------------------------------------------

There is no CI anywhere in this repository: no workflow, no pipeline
configuration, no commit hook. Every check named above and in BUILDING.md runs
only when a person runs it, on the machine they run it on. A reader who sees a
regression suite, a pinned-output check and a documentation check will
reasonably assume something enforces them on each commit, and nothing does.
The per-change expected values of the "Product-dependency status" section below
are therefore a manual procedure, not an automated gate, and no record exists
of which commits the suite was run against.

ea_non_iid, ea_iid and ea_restart are built from this fork; the repairs
listed above and the regression suite cover all three. ea_conditioning,
the only program that links MPFR and GMP, is not built or shipped, and
none of the three that are built links either: verified with otool -L
and nm -u on each binary, and cpp/Makefile still passes COND_LIB only
in the ea_conditioning rule.


