Product-dependency status (recorded 2026-10-01)
-----------------------------------------------

This patched build is no longer only an upstream proposal: TruePad SHIPS it as
the entropy-assessment engine of its hardware-RNG path. That changes four things,
recorded here because this file is now part of the evidence chain, not a licence
formality.

1. THE EVIDENCE SENTENCE CHANGES. "Assessed with NIST's reference
   implementation" is no longer accurate for a shipped patched build. The honest
   form names the patched build, its commit, and that each change is documented
   above and was offered upstream with its issue number. Every surface that
   states the unpatched form must be updated to the patched form, and the dated
   change list above is the artefact that backs it: it is read as evidence, not
   skimmed as a formality.

2. THE BAR RISES. A fix that upstream declines costs nothing as a PR; shipped, a
   wrong fix silently changes the number pads are priced against. TruePad's
   independent review therefore now covers a PRODUCT DEPENDENCY, and any fix the
   review cannot independently support DOES NOT SHIP, whatever its claimed
   upstream status (accepted, "a real bug", or still open). Upstream acceptance
   is necessary context, not sufficient warrant.

3. THE PINNED-OUTPUT TEST'S JOB CHANGES. On an unpatched build the single pin
   (cpp/selftest/pin-check.sh on bin/ringOsc-nist.bin) proves equivalence to the
   reference. On a patched build that pin proves only that the UNCHANGED paths
   still match the reference; it cannot speak to the deliberate divergences. The
   requirement for a patched build is PER-CHANGE EXPECTED VALUES: each deliberate
   divergence (e.g. F09's MultiMMC rise) must have a recorded expected figure and
   be shown to produce exactly that, AND nothing else may have moved. The
   cpp/selftest/regression-*.sh scripts are that mechanism; the global pin is
   necessary but no longer sufficient.

4. THE ALTERNATIVE GIVEN UP. Shipping UNPATCHED keeps the evidence at its
   strongest — "assessed with NIST's own reference implementation, unmodified" —
   at the cost of carrying the known defects (the heap over-read, the
   divide-by-zero estimates, the sub-minimum-sample acceptance, and the rest
   listed above). Shipping PATCHED was chosen instead: the defects are real and
   several change the number in the dangerous direction, so carrying them into a
   product that prices pads on that number was judged worse than taking on the
   evidence burden of a documented, upstream-offered, independently-reviewed fork.
   This was a decision, not a default; a later reader should see both sides were
   weighed.
